# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. Matthew 24 opens with Jesus leaving the temple. “And Jesus went out, and departed from the temple: and his disciples came to him for to shew him the buildings of the temple” (24:1). Jesus tells them, “There shall not be left here one stone upon another, that shall not be thrown down” (24:2). Then, “as he sat upon the mount of Olives, the disciples came unto him privately, saying, Tell us, when shall these things be? and what shall be the sign of thy coming, and of the end of the world?” (24:3). Jesus begins His answer, “Take heed that no man deceive you” (24:4).'))
A(('p', '2.2 Where this parable stands. The talents come near the close of that answer. Jesus has said, “Watch therefore: for ye know not what hour your Lord doth come” (24:42), and He has spoken of a faithful and wise servant and an evil servant (24:45–51). Matthew then records the ten virgins, which end with the words, “Watch therefore, for ye know neither the day nor the hour wherein the Son of man cometh” (25:13). The talents begin directly after: “For the kingdom of heaven is as a man travelling into a far country” (25:14). Matthew does not say what “For” joins to. After the story, the next words are, “When the Son of man shall come in his glory” (25:31), and the next chapter opens, “when Jesus had finished all these sayings” (26:1).'))
A(('call', 'The Question Stands Chapters Before the Parable',
   'The disciples’ question is recorded at 24:3. No question or remark from a hearer is recorded between 25:13 and 25:14. Matthew does not say that the talents answer the question of 24:3, and this study adds nothing to that.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The setting before the scene. Jesus has just spoken over Jerusalem: “Behold, your house is left unto you desolate” (23:38). The talents are told on the Mount of Olives to disciples who came to Him privately (24:3). Matthew gives no other hearers for this parable.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. Matthew has said what Jesus preached: “From that time Jesus began to preach, and to say, Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (4:17). He sent the Twelve with a limited charge: “Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. And as ye go, preach, saying, The kingdom of heaven is at hand” (10:5–7). The talents use the same words, “the kingdom of heaven” (25:14).'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells it here. Matthew gives no statement of why Jesus told this parable. What he gives is the place, the Mount of Olives; the disciples’ question (24:3); the words “Watch therefore” just before it (25:13); the words that follow (25:31); and the close of the sayings (26:1). The talents are not a free-floating saying about money or gifts. They belong to the answer Jesus gives to His disciples on the mount, and they are read there.'))
