A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Minas, the Faithful and Unfaithful Servant, the Ten Virgins and the Sheep and the Goats are each read in their own study.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Jesus compares the kingdom of heaven to a man travelling into a far country who delivered his goods to his own servants, five talents, two and one, each by his ability. Two servants traded and gained, and one hid his lord’s money. After a long time the lord came and reckoned. The first two heard “Well done, thou good and faithful servant,” and the third was called wicked and slothful. His talent was taken and given to him who had ten, and the unprofitable servant was cast into outer darkness. No explanation is recorded, and Jesus names none of the parts. The comparison says that what is entrusted is to be used, that the master’s absence is followed by a return, that faithfulness is answered with more, that fear is not an excuse, and that what is not used is taken away.'))
